0 KineBrick corrections to the connection table
0 // Snap lines for what neither the LDraw parts nor the LDCad shadow library describe.
0 // Each "0 FILE" block applies to one part, after that part's shadow lines.
0 // Same syntax as the LDCad shadow library, plus extra section shapes (T, a tyre seat; K, a
0 // clutch; B, a bore a part may slide along) and extra line types (!KINEBRICK GEAR: a gear's
0 // tooth count, pitch radius and tooth width, and for bevel gears which side its bevel teeth
0 // face and how far away its pitch cone's tip is; !KINEBRICK SLIDE for parts that click into
0 // set places; !KINEBRICK TRACK for track links, and how far they reach out beyond the line
0 // through their pins).

0 FILE 3482.dat
0 // Wheel Rim 8 x 17.5 with Axlehole: tyre seat around its outside, 20 LDU wide
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=T 22 20] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3739.dat
0 // Wheel 24 x 43 Technic (the big wheel of early tractors and cars): a tyre seat around the
0 // middle of its axle hole, which runs along x
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=T 30 20] [center=true] [pos=-30 0 0] [ori=0 -1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1]

0 FILE 3740.dat
0 // Tyre 24/ 80 x 43 Offset Tread: sits on the big Technic wheel (3739)
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=T 30 20] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3483.dat
0 // Tyre 56 x 17 Offset Tread: sits on a 22 LDU radius rim seat, 18 LDU wide
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=T 22 18] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3634.dat
0 // Tyre 10/130 x 17 Offset Tread: a big tyre for the same rims (3482), its tread offset towards -z
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=T 22 18] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 // Spur gears. Pitch radius is 1.25 LDU per tooth, so two meshing gears' axles are
0 // (teeth1 + teeth2) x 1.25 LDU apart. Every gear's axis runs along z, teeth 9.5 LDU wide.

0 FILE 3647.dat
0 // Technic Gear 8 Tooth
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=8] [radius=10] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 4019.dat
0 // Technic Gear 16 Tooth
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=16] [radius=20] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3648b.dat
0 // Technic Gear 24 Tooth with Single Axle Hole (3648 is an alias for this part)
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3648a.dat
0 // Technic Gear 24 Tooth with 3 Axleholes (the old 24 tooth gear)
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 // Crown gears: a 24 tooth gear whose teeth also point along its axis, towards +z, so a spur
0 // gear of the same tooth size meshes with them on an axle crossing its own at right angles.
0 // Their pitch plane is 3 LDU along from the middle, measured from how official models mount
0 // them (8 tooth gears' centres 13 LDU out from the middle, 24 tooth gears' 33). The same teeth
0 // also mesh side by side, as a spur gear's.

0 FILE 3650a.dat
0 // Technic Gear 24 Tooth Crown Type 1
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=6] [crown=+] [center=true] [pos=0 0 3] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3650b.dat
0 // Technic Gear 24 Tooth Crown Type 2
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=6] [crown=+] [center=true] [pos=0 0 3] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3650c.dat
0 // Technic Gear 24 Tooth Crown Type 3
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=6] [crown=+] [center=true] [pos=0 0 3] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 3649.dat
0 // Technic Gear 40 Tooth
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=40] [radius=50] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 // Bevel gears. Bevel teeth lie on a 45 degree cone; two bevel gears on crossing axles mesh
0 // when their cones' tips meet where the axles cross. Measured from the LDraw shapes: for
0 // double bevel gears, tooth radius + distance along the axle is constant (the cone),
0 // reaching the axle 20, 30, 40, 50 LDU from the middle for 12, 20, 28, 36 teeth.
0 // Double bevel gears also mesh side by side like spur gears, with the same size of tooth:
0 // 1.25 LDU pitch radius per tooth (their teeth reach 17 and 27 LDU out for 12 and 20 teeth).

0 FILE 32270.dat
0 // Technic Gear 12 Tooth Double Bevel
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=12] [radius=15] [width=20] [bevel=both] [apex=20] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 32269.dat
0 // Technic Gear 20 Tooth Double Bevel
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=20] [radius=25] [width=20] [bevel=both] [apex=30] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 6589.dat
0 // Technic Gear 12 Tooth Bevel: teeth face +z, cone tip 17 LDU out (where it sits in a differential)
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=12] [radius=12] [width=7] [spur=false] [bevel=+] [apex=17] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 6573.dat
0 // Technic Differential with Gear 16 Tooth and 24 Tooth: a 24 tooth ring at z = 30, a 16 tooth
0 // ring at z = -30, and a post inside for the middle (planet) bevel gear to turn on. The
0 // shadow library gives the post a closed end; it's open here so the gear's hole can take it.
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=9.5] [center=true] [pos=0 0 30] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=16] [radius=20] [width=9.5] [center=true] [pos=0 0 -30] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=R 4 8] [pos=0 20 0]

0 // Worm gears. A worm's thread is a single right-handed helix; each turn moves a meshing gear
0 // on by one tooth, so its lead (how far the thread advances per turn) is one gear tooth:
0 // 2 x pi x 1.25 = 7.853982 LDU. Measured from the LDraw shape. It meshes like an 8 tooth gear
0 // (pitch radius 10) with a spur gear whose axle crosses it at right angles.

0 FILE 4716.dat
0 // Technic Worm Gear 2L
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=1] [radius=10] [width=40] [spur=false] [worm=7.853982] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 // Gearboxes (the 3L driving ring system). A ridged 3L axle joiner (18948) joins two axles;
0 // clutch gears spin freely on those axles either side of it. A 3L driving ring (18947) slides
0 // on the joiner, locked to it for turning, and clicks into three places: centred (neutral) or
0 // half a stud (10 LDU) either way. Shifted, the clutch teeth at one end reach 8 LDU into the
0 // neighbouring gear's clutch face, and that gear turns with the joiner. Measured from the LDraw
0 // shapes: the ring's teeth fill its last 8 LDU at each end; a gear's clutch face is the outer
0 // 4 LDU of each side.

0 FILE 18947.dat
0 // Technic Transmission Driving Ring 3L: clutch teeth at both ends, and its three places
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=K 12 8] [pos=0 0 28] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 -1 0 1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=K 12 8] [pos=0 0 -28] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]
0 !KINEBRICK SLIDE [positions=-10 0 10] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 18946.dat
0 // Technic Gear 16 Tooth with Clutch on Both Sides
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=16] [radius=20] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=K 12 4] [pos=0 0 10] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 -1 0 1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=K 12 4] [pos=0 0 -10] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 FILE 2471.dat
0 // Technic Gear 24 Tooth with Clutch on Both Sides
0 !KINEBRICK GEAR [teeth=24] [radius=30] [width=9.5] [center=true] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=K 12 4] [pos=0 0 10] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 -1 0 1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=K 12 4] [pos=0 0 -10] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 // Engine parts.

0 FILE 2850a.dat
0 // Technic Engine Cylinder Head with Side Slots: the shadow library's lines, with the bore
0 // (where the piston goes) marked as one a part may slide along (B) instead of round (R).
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CLEAR
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=R 8 2   R 6 16   R 8 4   R 6 16   R 8 2] [center=true] [pos=0 26 0] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 -1 0 1 0] [grid=C 2 1 40 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=one] [secs=R 6 4] [grid=C 2 C 2 20 20]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=B 12 47] [pos=0 43 0]

0 FILE 2853a.dat
0 // Technic Engine Crankshaft with Slot: an axle hole at x = 0 (z from -20 to -10, through
0 // the crank's plate), and the crank pin, an axle stub half a stud away at x = -10 (z from
0 // -10 to 20). Measured from the LDraw shape. The shadow library has no file for this part;
0 // the primitives it's built from would give the crank pin a closed end, so no rod could
0 // go on it, so those are cleared.
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CLEAR
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=F] [caps=none] [secs=A 6 10] [center=true] [pos=0 0 -15] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=A 6 30] [center=true] [pos=-10 0 5] [ori=1 0 0 0 0 1 0 -1 0]

0 // Racks. A rack's teeth are the same size as a spur gear's: one gear tooth (2 x pi x 1.25 =
0 // 7.854 LDU) apart, though the LDraw shapes draw them 8 LDU apart. !KINEBRICK RACK gives the
0 // pitch line (where a gear's pitch circle touches it): where it starts [pos], which way it
0 // runs [dir] and how long it is, which way the teeth face [face], and how wide they are.

0 FILE 3743.dat
0 // Technic Gear Rack 1 x 4: a plate with ten teeth along its top, their tips 6 LDU above it. Its pitch line is 4 LDU above its top, so an 8 tooth gear in a Technic brick's hole
0 // meshes with a rack lying on plates, as LEGO designed it.
0 !KINEBRICK RACK [teeth=10] [length=80] [width=20] [pos=-40 -4 0] [dir=1 0 0] [face=0 -1 0]

0 // Sliding on tiles. A plate resting on smooth tiles slides along them (rails either side keep
0 // it in line; KineBrick doesn't check for those), for as long as it still overlaps the row of
0 // tiles by a stud. The plate's underside is a slider (S) along its length, and each tile's top
0 // a plain surface (P) it may slide on.

0 FILE 3460.dat
0 // Plate 1 x 8
0 !LDCAD SNAP_CYL [gender=M] [caps=none] [secs=S 10 160] [center=true] [pos=0 8 0] [ori=0 -1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1]

